UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 5, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is two or three sentences saying what you made, which elements you used and what you want the viewer to feel. Viewers respond politely with two stars, which are two things that worked well, and a wish, which is one idea to improve. Handle other people''s work with care and keep your comments kind and specific.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a two-sentence artist statement for one piece you made this unit, then write two stars and a wish another person could give you.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look friendly'' and ''The yellow door stands out.'' Wish: ''Add a texture to the footpath.'' It names the elements, the feeling and a polite suggestion.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose your best three pieces from this unit and make a mini gallery at home with a label for each. Write an artist statement for each. Invite a family member to give two stars and a wish for one piece, and write one change you would make.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is two or three sentences saying what you made, which elements you used and what you want the viewer to feel. Viewers respond politely with two stars, which are two things that worked well, and a wish, which is one idea to improve. Handle other people''s work with care and keep your comments kind and specific.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose your best three pieces from this unit and make a mini gallery at home with a label for each. Write an artist statement for each. Invite a family member to give two stars and a wish for one piece, and write one cha Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look friendly'' and ''The yellow door stands out.''… Reasoning: I used a showcase by attending to an artist statement rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve. Correction: Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a showcase.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look… This matches the lesson skill for a showcase.","options":["Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look…"],"question":"Write a two-sentence artist statement for one piece you made this unit, then write two stars and a wish another person could give you. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is…","Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look…","Careful use of an artist statement with clear evidence.","Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named an artist statement, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the an artist statement used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a showcase. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look…","Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a showcase.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about an artist statement?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: Tara''s label reads: ''Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel quiet. I want the…","Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve.","Choose your best three pieces from this unit and make a mini gallery at home with a label for each. Write an artist statement for each. Invite a…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look friendly'' and ''The yellow door stands out.''… Reasoning: I used a showcase by attending to an artist statement rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve. Correction: Strong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a showcase.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w05-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a showcase.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use a showcase with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify an artist statement and explain why it matters.","I can practise two stars and a wish independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a display needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is two or three sentences saying what you made, which elements you used and what you want the viewer to feel. Viewers…","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is two or three sentences saying what you made, which elements you used and what you want the viewer to feel. Viewers respond politely with two stars, which are two things that worked well, and a wish, which is one idea to improve. Handle other people''s work with care and keep your comments kind and specific.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Tara''s label reads: ''Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel quiet. I want the viewer to feel cosy.'' A visitor says, ''Star: the grey zigzag lines look like heavy rain. Star: I feel cosy because of the warm lit window. Wish: add a puddle texture.'' The comments name specific elements.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a two-sentence artist statement for one piece you made this unit, then write two stars and a wish another person could give you.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look friendly'' and ''The yellow door stands out.'' Wish: ''Add a texture to the footpath.'' It names the elements, the feeling and a polite suggestion.","explain":"At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is two or three sentences saying what you made, which elements you used and what you want the viewer to feel. Viewers respond politely with two stars, which are two things that worked well, and a wish, which is one idea to improve. Handle other people''s work with care and keep your comments kind and specific.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"An artist statement and label.","caption":"Say what you made.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Statement</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">what, elements, feeling</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Label</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">title and name</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Tara''s label reads: ''Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel q Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Tara''s label reads: ''Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel q Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Tara''s label reads: ''Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel quiet. I want the viewer to feel cosy.'' A visitor says, ''Star: the grey zigzag lines look like heavy rain. Star: I feel cosy because of the warm lit window. Wish: add a puddle texture.'' The comments name specific elements. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two stars and a wish.","caption":"Specific and kind.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Star</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grey zigzag looks like rain</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wish</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">add a puddle texture</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Tara''s label reads: ''Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel quiet. I want the viewer to feel cosy.'' A visitor says, ''Star: the grey zigzag lines look like heavy rain. Star: I feel cosy because of the warm lit window. Wish: add a puddle texture.'' The comments name specific elements.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.'' Stars: ''The curved fence lines look friendly'' and ''The yellow door stands out.'' Wish: ''Add a texture to the footpath.'' It names the elements, the feeling and a polite suggestion.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAt the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is two or three sentences saying what you made, which elements you used and what you want the viewer to feel. Viewers respond politely with two stars, which are two things that worked well, and a wish, which is one idea to improve. Handle other people''s work with care and keep your comments kind and specific. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 5, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Drawing, line, shape and colour","week":5,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Tara''s label reads: ''Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel quiet. I want the viewer to feel cosy.'' A visitor says, ''Star: the grey zigzag lines look like heavy rain. Star: I feel cosy because of the warm lit window. Wish: add a puddle texture.'' The comments name specific elements.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"An artist statement and label.","caption":"Say what you made.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Statement</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">what, elements, feeling</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Label</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">title and name</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two stars and a wish.","caption":"Specific and kind.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Star</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grey zigzag looks like rain</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wish</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">add a puddle texture</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w05-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
